  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThe main focus of this forecast period continues to be the potential \nfor near-record heat from Monday through Thursday, and possibly into \nFriday as well. As discussed in the short term period, a stout upper \nlevel ridge will build overhead Sunday and is expected to remain in \nplace for several days thereafter, with 98+ percentile 500 mb \ngeopotential heights and 850 mb temperatures, along with downsloping \nsouth-southwest surface winds. While dewpoints should increase very \nslightly over the course of the week, they should remain in the 60s \nearly in the week and perhaps only approach the low 70s. While this \nisn't a desert airmass by any stretch, this should be low enough to \nkeep humidity levels (and likewise heat index values) in check. Add \nin some afternoon breezes thanks to deep mixing, and this should \nhelp to mitigate the heat slightly on each individual day. \n\nOn the other hand though, confidence remains high that actual \ntemperatures will approach the 100 degree mark each day from Monday \nthrough Thursday. While not unprecedented, this is relatively late \nin the summer for such high temperatures, and daily records \ngenerally range from 100 to 104 degrees during that span at all\nof our local climate stations. Not only this, but current model\nforecasts for low temperatures forecasts during this period are\nalso near record \"warm minimum\" values (mid 70s to near 80). \nThis might be the most surprising aspect of the forecast given \nthat humidity levels are not especially high, although \npersistent southwest low level winds may be contributing to \nthis. Still, there is some slight skepticism in these values, \nand wind-protected areas are likely to see a bit more overnight \nrelief than that. Still, ECMWF Extreme Forecast Index (EFI) \nvalues exceed .95 for both the max and min temperatures during \nthis period with a non-trivial \"Shift of Tails\" (1-2), adding \nconfidence that we are looking at an anomalously warm, near \nrecord temperature event once you stack it all up.\n\nAt this point, while actual temperatures are forecast to be near 100 \ndegrees for several days, confidence is relatively low (30-50%) that \nwe will see 105+ degree heat index values for 4 or more\nconsecutive days, and even lower that we will reach 110 degree \nheat index values on any individual day (Extreme Heat Warning \ncriteria). One area to watch closely, though, will be the urban \nheat island of the St. Louis metro area, and strictly based on \nNBM deterministic forecast values, we get very close (just a \ncouple of degrees short on Monday) to meeting the bare minimum \nfor a duration-driven Heat Warning over a small footprint. \nConsidering the relatively low humidity, uncertainty surrounding\nthe morning low temperatures, and increasing ensemble \ntemperature spreads later in the event, we will refrain from \nhoisting an Extreme Heat Watch for the time being. It should be \nnoted, though, that it is very likely that lower-level Heat \nAdvisories will be needed in at least parts of the area as we \nget a little bit closer to the event.\n\nOtherwise, there appears to be very little chance for meaningful \nprecipitation through at least Wednesday, but we do start to see \nensemble probabilities creep up into the 20-30% range Thursday \nthrough the end of the week as the ridge weakens and a weak front \nsinks south. As noted previously, NBM/LREF temperature spreads \nincrease considerably during this period as a result, offering \nsome hope that the worst of the heat may subside slightly late \nin the week even if we remain above average for several more \ndays.",
